=== Git in Visual Studio Code
tag::concept[]

(((Visual Studio Code)))
Visual Studio Code (VS Code) is a popular choice for designers who also work with design tokens, CSS, or documentation. For Sam, the UI designer at SketchSpark, VS Code isn't just an editor—it’s where he manages his design-to-code workflow.

#### Built-in Support
tag::procedure[]

VS Code has excellent Git support built in. If Sam opens the SketchSpark repo in VS Code, he gets several powerful features:

*   **Gutter Diffs:** As Sam edits `design-tokens.json`, he sees small color-coded indicators in the left margin (the "gutter") showing exactly which lines he has added, modified, or deleted.
*   **Source Control View:** A dedicated sidebar shows all pending changes. Sam can stage individual files or even individual lines of code by clicking the **+** icon.
*   **Status Bar:** At a glance, Sam can see which branch he is on, whether he has unpushed commits, and if there are updates on the server.

#### GitHub Integration
tag::procedure[]

By installing the **GitHub Pull Requests and Issues** extension, Sam can handle the entire "GitHub Flow" inside his editor. He can open PRs, read Nora's feedback, and respond to comments without ever switching to a web browser.

This deep integration makes Git feel like a natural extension of the design process rather than a separate chore.
